.TH V32AS-INC 3 "2026-11-26" "v32as 20261126-dev" "Vircon32 assembler manual"
.SH NAME
INC, DEC, ZERO, NEG, CLAMP, SWAP, PUSHALL, POPALL, WAIT_FRAMES \- helper macros for v32as (lib/v32as.inc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B %include "v32as.inc"
.sp
.BI "INC " "reg" " [, " n "]"
.BI "DEC " "reg" " [, " n "]"
.BI "ZERO " "reg" ", ..."
.BI "NEG " "reg"
.BI "CLAMP " "reg" ", " lo ", " hi
.BI "SWAP " "a" ", " b
.BI "PUSHALL " "reg" ", ..."
.BI "POPALL " "reg" ", ..."
.BI "WAIT_FRAMES " "[n]"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I lib/v32as.inc
is a library of small statement macros written with the
.B v32as
preprocessor (see
.BR v32as\-language (7)).
They need the extended preprocessor, so they cannot be used with
.BR "v32as \-\-standard" .
An installed v32as, or one unpacked from a release zip, finds the library by
itself; from the source tree, put its directory on the include path:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-I lib game.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
and in the source
.PP
.nf
.RS
%include <v32as.inc>
.RE
.fi
.PP
The file starts with
.BR %includeonce ,
so including it twice is harmless.
Every macro checks its operands with the
.B %if
tests, so a mistake is reported at the line that made it, followed by a note
naming the macro, for example
.PP
.nf
.RS
game.asm:2: error: INC: the first operand must be a register
game.asm:2: note: in expansion of macro 'INC' (defined at lib/v32as.inc:21)
.RE
.fi
.PP
A \(lqregister\(rq here means a CPU register (R0 to R15, or the aliases CR, SR,
DR, BP, SP, in either case); the internal registers IP, IR and IV are not
accepted.
Macro names are case\-sensitive, so they are written in capitals.
Each macro expands to ordinary instructions, so the usual rules about
instruction operands apply (for instance, the immediate forms of the arithmetic
instructions).
.SH MACROS
.TP
.BI "INC " "reg" " [, " n "]"
Add
.I n
to
.IR reg ;
.I n
defaults to 1.
Expands to
.BI "iadd " "reg" ", " n \fR.
.TP
.BI "DEC " "reg" " [, " n "]"
Subtract
.I n
from
.IR reg ;
.I n
defaults to 1.
Expands to
.BI "isub " "reg" ", " n \fR.
.TP
.BI "ZERO " "reg" ", ..."
Set each register listed to 0 (one
.B "mov reg, 0"
each).
At least one register is needed; every operand must be a register.
.TP
.BI "NEG " "reg"
Negate an integer register: expands to
.BI "imul " "reg" ", \-1" \fR.
.TP
.BI "CLAMP " "reg" ", " lo ", " hi
Keep an integer register between
.I lo
and
.IR hi :
expands to
.B imax
.IR reg ", " lo
followed by
.B imin
.IR reg ", " hi .
All three arguments are required.
.TP
.BI "SWAP " "a" ", " b
Exchange two registers.
It uses the stack (it expands to
.B push a
.B mov a, b
.B pop b )
and so needs the stack pointer set up.
Swapping a register with a textually identical one expands to nothing.
.TP
.BI "PUSHALL " "reg" ", ..."
Push each register, in the order given.
.TP
.BI "POPALL " "reg" ", ..."
Pop the registers in reverse order, so that
.B POPALL
takes the same list as the matching
.BR PUSHALL .
.TP
.BI "WAIT_FRAMES " "[n]"
Wait
.I n
frames (default 1): expands to
.I n
.B wait
instructions.
.I n
must be a constant expression the preprocessor can evaluate (it is the count of
a
.BR %rep ),
not a register.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
%include "v32as.inc"
_main:
    INC     R0
    INC     R1, 4
    ZERO    R3, R4
    CLAMP   R6, 0, 255
    SWAP    R7, R8
    PUSHALL R1, R2
    POPALL  R1, R2
    WAIT_FRAMES 2
.RE
.fi
.PP
expands (as shown by
.BR "v32as \-E \-P" )
to
.PP
.nf
.RS
_main:
    iadd  R0, 1
    iadd  R1, 4
    mov   R3, 0
    mov   R4, 0
    imax  R6, 0
    imin  R6, 255
    push  R7
    mov   R7, R8
    pop   R8
    push  R1
    push  R2
    pop   R2
    pop   R1
    wait
    wait
.RE
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An operand that is not a register is reported with
.BR "INC: the first operand must be a register" ,
.BR "ZERO: every operand must be a register" ,
and so on, one message for each macro.
A wrong number of arguments is reported by the preprocessor, for example
.BR "macro 'SWAP' takes 2 arguments, but 1 given" .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR v32as (1),
.BR v32as\-control (3),
.BR v32as\-language (7)
